---
import { site } from "../../data/site";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const lede =
  "A firewall that sits between applications and an LLM, screening prompts for attacks and responses for harmful output.";

const facts = [
  { term: "Year", detail: "2024" },
  { term: "Team", detail: "Four, built alongside national service" },
  { term: "My part", detail: "Full-stack developer" },
  { term: "Stack", detail: "Svelte and Supabase, with Python for evaluation" },
  {
    term: "Result",
    detail: "1st place, NSCC HPC Innovation Competition 2023, university category",
  },
];

const links = [
  { label: "Garak PR", href: "https://github.com/NVIDIA/garak/pull/572" },
  { label: "Competition", href: "https://www.nscc.sg" },
];

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "CreativeWork",
  name: "OwlShield",
  description: lede,
  dateCreated: "2024",
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: site.name, url: Astro.site?.href },
};
---

<Base title="OwlShield" description={lede} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="flex flex-col gap-12">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-3.5">
      <a
        href="/#section-projects"
        class="self-start text-meta text-faint no-underline hover:underline"
      >
        <span aria-hidden="true">←{" "}</span>Projects
      </a>
      <h1 class="text-title font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em]">OwlShield</h1>
      <p class="text-lede text-pretty text-ink2">{lede}</p>
    </header>

    <dl
      class="grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,96px)_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-x-4 gap-y-2.5 border-y border-rule py-[18px] text-base"
    >
      {facts.map((f) => (
  <>
    <dt class="text-faint">{f.term}</dt>
    <dd>{f.detail}</dd>
  </>
))}
    </dl>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <h2 class="text-base text-faint italic">The problem</h2>
      <p class="text-pretty">
        Teams shipping LLM features have little between the user and the model. Prompt injection and
        jailbreaks go straight in; unsafe or leaked content comes straight out. Most mitigations are
        baked into each application, so every team rebuilds them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <h2 class="text-base text-faint italic">How it works</h2>
      <p class="text-pretty">
        OwlShield runs as a proxy in front of the model. Each request passes through input checks
        before it reaches the LLM, and each response through output checks before it reaches the
        user. Policies are configured once and apply to every app behind the proxy, which gives
        teams one place to keep their apps safe and in line with AI regulations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <h2 class="text-base text-faint italic">Testing it</h2>
      <p class="text-pretty">
        To measure what OwlShield caught, we wanted to run Garak, NVIDIA's open-source LLM
        vulnerability scanner, against it. I wrote a LiteLLM integration for Garak so its probes
        could reach OwlShield, and contributed it upstream; it was merged in April 2024.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <h2 class="text-base text-faint italic">The competition</h2>
      <p class="text-pretty">
        We built OwlShield for the HPC Innovation Competition 2023, run by Singapore's National
        Supercomputing Centre (NSCC). The work ran from January to July 2024, and the team won first
        place in the university category.
      </p>
    </section>

    <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 pt-2 text-base">
      {links.map((l) => (
  <li>
    <a href={l.href}>{l.label}</a>
  </li>
))}
    </ul>

    <a
      href="/#section-projects"
      class="self-start text-meta text-faint no-underline hover:underline"
    >
      <span aria-hidden="true">←{" "}</span>All projects
    </a>
  </article>
</Base>
